Z||000000||kanada||04-11-2001||02:00 PM||||just wondering what engines to look for to replace in my 1986 se 2.8<P>the engine code is 9, but i was wondering if all the 2.8 gm engines (x, w,s) from this erra would fit, and if so what about the tranny||||reg||9
Z||000001||fropuff||04-11-2001||02:22 PM||||If you want a little more power, you can do the 3.4L pushrod swap.  The engines come from the 93-95 Camaros/Firebirds and put out around 160 HP w/ no mods.  The Fiero intake and computer can also be used, raising the HP and making the 3.4 look almost exactly like the stock 2.8.  A few mods must be done to the block itself, but Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory does these.  His shop is in Toney, AL.  I think this is a worth-while swap to perform.  I'm thinking of doing this swap this summer.  Just a thought.  <P>PS:  Welcome to the forum!<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||04-11-2001||03:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>The Fiero intake and computer can also be used, raising the HP and making the 3.4 look almost exactly like the stock 2.8.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe this is a dumb question, but how does the computer know that you have a bigger engine (or larger bore throttle body)? Doesn't the Fiero engine measure the MAP only?<BR><P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||5
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-11-2001||04:33 PM||||The W block will work, the X & S will not (starter on wrong side)<BR>The Fiero heads need to retained for the W block as well as all intake componets as well as the distrubtor.<BR>All brackets and front-end parts of the engine also must be Fiero to be used on the W.<BR>Transmission, depends on what you want.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kanada||04-11-2001||04:54 PM||||i'm unsure of what you mean by "fiero head need to retain"<P>do all w engines fit or just specific years<p>[This message has been edited by kanada (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||kanada||04-11-2001||05:54 PM||||what modifications have to be made to the block of the 3.4l||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||04-11-2001||08:30 PM||||The Fiero heads need to retained . . . .<P>Simply put, you need the heads off the Fiero engine to put on the 'W' block as they are different. Giving it more thought, the pistons need to be swapped (changed from one to engine the other) as well.<P>To put it another way, the entire top, front and I think pistons from the Fiero (Vin 9) engine need to be transplanted to the 'W' block!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dave Gunsul||04-11-2001||09:04 PM||||You can also buy a 3.1 stroker kit.<BR>Then all you have to do is have your orig-<BR>inal engine rebuilt using this kit.<BR>If you do this get a crane or Comp. Cams<BR>cam for some more performance. This is also<BR>a good time to add some other improvements<BR>as well.(to many to list here)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The 3.4 swap is also a good swap but you <BR>really should have this done buy a Fiero<BR>type shop as there are several changes that<BR>have to be made.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000008||fropuff||04-11-2001||10:53 PM||||Here is what it said on Ed Parks' site:<P>"We had a drill guide plate manufactured that allows drilling and tapping starter mounting holes on an assembled 3.4 longblock.  There are a few easy bracket modifications to do or that we furnish.  The 3.4 needed is used in 93-95 Camaro's and Firebird's.  Larger flow rate injectors have to be used and don't come with the "crate" engine.  Would have to be purchased separately.  Must use the 3.4 injectors, or 305 chevy (both will "clip" to the Fiero fuel rail).  We also use an adjustable fuel regulator to be on the safe side.  There are a few other minor mods as well as the major one of changing starter sides.  If you are auto, part of the 3.4 needs to be cutaway to clear the trans.  The bolt hole that takes the bolt from the pass side, lowest bellhousing one toward the trunk, is threaded in the 3.4 and needs to be drilled smooth.  The oil pressure sensor tube needs an adapter, 3.4 blocks are minus one of the bosses that the engine shock mounts to.  That boss is also where the a/c adjusting bracket bolts.  Cure is: shock mount bracket with bolt welded from the back to become a stud.  88's will then need 85-87 a/c bracket. A modified 85-87 front engine mount bracket must be used on the 88 engine cradle if you use the 3.4 oil pan. 87-88 timing covers will work with 3.4 pan, otherwise use cover and pan from 85-86 2.8. 3.4 dampner needs to aligned with the 2.8, and new timing mark cut. Lower bellhousing bolt hole (trans side) is threaded on 3.4. Drill out threads for bolt coming from opposite direction to transaxle.  Reducer bushing necessary for oil sensor tube.  Clip wires from 3.4 cam and crank sensor and leave installed, is easiest way to plug these holes.  Remove 3.4 oil cooler, and install 2.8 filter adapter. 3.4's need neutrally balanced flexplate/flywheel which 88 2.8's already have.  A 4-5 lb heavier flywheel available for 3.4TDC from Chev for about 1/3 the cost of 88 Fiero. (They are identical except for weight).  An 87-88 Fiero timing chain cover will work with the 3.4 oil pan.  You would need to use both your 85 or 86 pan and cover, as a set.  It also requires a Fiero water pump instead of the 3.4's."<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||04-12-2001||06:29 PM||||Indiana: The Fiero 2.8 pistons will NOT work with a 3.4L. The 3.4 is a bored and stroked 60Deg GM v6. The fiero pistons will be quite a bit smaller.<P>According to Ed Parks, in some email correspondance, just switch the block over, attach all the fiero components. (Heads, Exhaust, Valve covers, intake, Distributer, etc...) The starter mounts will have to be drilled and tapped (Ed will do this for you),  one of the flanges on the engine block must be trimmed down, and one of the mounting brackets need to be modified.<BR>As for the computer, it does not really measure fuel (X% o2 = Xml of fuel) but rather timed pulses. It doesn't know how big the block is, or care. It's goal is to make the O2 sensor return a certain value, and adjusts fuel pulse accordingly. The Fiero injectors are too small. Use the 3.4's or some from a 305, and install a variable fuel pressure regulator. According to Ed, these will bolt up. Since more fuel will come out per pulse, the computer will be able to cope.||||reg||12
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||04-12-2001||11:27 PM||||Mach10,<P>READ THE POST!<BR>That remark was a clarification to an earlier post that kanada didn't understand in relation to a 2.8! W, X & S blocks from the opening ?.<P>I was born at night, but not last night.<BR>Thank you very much!<BR>||||reg||1
